## Answer

**Yes** — a continuous function can be defined from the irrationals to the even integers. In fact, a **non-constant** one exists (constant functions being trivial).

---

### Proof

Let $\mathbb{I} = \mathbb{R}\setminus\mathbb{Q}$ with the subspace topology, and let $2\mathbb{Z}$ carry the discrete topology (it is discrete as a subspace of $\mathbb{R}$, since each even integer $2k$ is isolated by $(2k-\tfrac12,\,2k+\tfrac12)\cap 2\mathbb{Z}=\{2k\}$).

**Key fact.** $\mathbb{I}$ has a basis of clopen sets.

*Proof of key fact.* Take any irrational $x$ and any open neighborhood $U$ of $x$ in $\mathbb{I}$. There is an open interval $(a,b)\subset\mathbb{R}$ with $x\in(a,b)$ and $(a,b)\cap\mathbb{I}\subset U$. Since $\mathbb{Q}$ is dense, shrink to rational endpoints $a',b'\in\mathbb{Q}$ with $a'<x<b'$. Set $V=(a',b')\cap\mathbb{I}$. Then $V$ is open in $\mathbb{I}$. It is also closed in $\mathbb{I}$: the closure of $(a',b')$ in $\mathbb{R}$ is $[a',b']$, and because $a',b'\in\mathbb{Q}$,
$$[a',b']\cap\mathbb{I}=(a',b')\cap\mathbb{I}=V.$$
So $V$ is clopen. Hence $\mathbb{I}$ is zero-dimensional (clopen basis). $\square$

**Construction.** Define $f:\mathbb{I}\to 2\mathbb{Z}$ by
$$
f(x)=\begin{cases}0,& x\in(0,1)\cap\mathbb{I},\\[2pt]2,& x\in\mathbb{I}\setminus\bigl((0,1)\cap\mathbb{I}\bigr).\end{cases}
$$

The set $A=(0,1)\cap\mathbb{I}$ is clopen in $\mathbb{I}$ by the key fact (the endpoints $0,1$ are rational, so $[0,1]\cap\mathbb{I}=(0,1)\cap\mathbb{I}=A$). Its complement $\mathbb{I}\setminus A$ is therefore clopen as well.

Since $2\mathbb{Z}$ is discrete, continuity of $f$ is equivalent to every fiber being open (equivalently clopen). Here
$$f^{-1}(\{0\})=A,\qquad f^{-1}(\{2\})=\mathbb{I}\setminus A,$$
both clopen. Thus $f$ is continuous, and it is non-constant since, e.g., $\sqrt2/2\in A$ has $f=0$ while $\sqrt2\notin(0,1)$ has $f=2$.

Therefore a continuous function from the set of irrational numbers to the set of even integers can be defined — indeed a non-constant one. $\blacksquare$

### PROOF COMPLETE
